steps.body:
**The big gotcha:** `sort()` with no comparator turns items into strings and compares them character by character, so `[10, 9, 1, 100].sort()` gives `1, 10, 100, 9`. For numbers, always pass a comparator.

```javascript harness
const nums = [10, 9, 1, 100];
const words = ["banana", "kiwi", "apple", "fig", "cherry"];
{{code}}
console.log(`nums: ${nums.join(", ")}`);
console.log(`words: ${words.join(", ")}`);
```

```javascript
nums.sort((a, b) => a - b); // ascending
nums.sort((a, b) => b - a); // descending
words.sort((a, b) => a.localeCompare(b)); // alphabetical
```

The comparator returns a negative number when `a` goes first, a positive number when `b` goes first, and `0` for a tie.

`sort` changes the array in place and returns that same array, so `const sorted = nums.sort(...)` is not a copy. `toSorted` takes the same comparator but returns a sorted copy, so use it when you still need the original order.

Sorting is **stable**: items that tie keep their original order. To break ties on purpose, chain a second comparison with `||`. A tie gives `0`, which is falsy, so `||` moves on to the next rule:

```javascript harness
const words = ["banana", "kiwi", "apple", "fig", "cherry"];
{{code}}
console.log(words.join(" "));
```

```javascript
words.sort((a, b) => a.length - b.length || a.localeCompare(b));
```

